# Zephyr — frontend

A plain HTML/CSS/JS frontend for the Zephyr comment-to-DM backend. No build step —
open the files in a browser or serve the folder with any static file server.

```
index.html            Landing page + "Connect Instagram" entry point
login-callback.html   Handles the Instagram OAuth redirect, exchanges the code for tokens
dashboard.html         Rules / Billing / Account
assets/config.js       API_BASE_URL + OAuth callback URL — edit this first
assets/api.js          Fetch wrapper: token storage, auto-refresh on 401, all endpoint calls
assets/ui.js           Toasts + small helpers
assets/style.css       Design tokens + shared styles
assets/landing.js, dashboard.js   Page-specific behaviour
```

## 1. Point it at your backend

Edit `assets/config.js`:

```js
window.ZEPHYR_CONFIG = {
  API_BASE_URL: "https://your-backend.example.com",
  OAUTH_CALLBACK_URL: window.location.origin + "/login-callback.html",
};
```

## 2. Backend changes this frontend needs

The API currently has no `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` headers, so browser
`fetch()` calls from a separately-hosted frontend will be blocked. Add CORS to
`main.py`:

```python
from fastapi.middleware.cors import CORSMiddleware

app.add_middleware(
    CORSMiddleware,
    allow_origins=["https://your-frontend.example.com"],  # no trailing slash
    allow_methods=["*"],
    allow_headers=["*"],
)
```

Also update two values so the OAuth and checkout redirects land back on the
frontend instead of the API:

- **`REDIRECT_URI` env var** (used in `routers/user.py`) → set it to
  `https://your-frontend.example.com/login-callback.html`, and register the
  same URL in your Instagram app's OAuth settings.
- **`routers/payments.py`**, `return_url` in `create_checkout` → currently
  hardcoded to `https://zephyr-m5w7.onrender.com/dashboard`. Point it at your
  frontend's dashboard page instead, e.g.
  `https://your-frontend.example.com/dashboard.html`.

### Optional but recommended: a `GET /user/me` endpoint

Right now the API never returns the signed-in user's `username`,
`profile_pic_url`, or subscription/trial dates — `instagram_callback` only
returns tokens. The dashboard currently falls back to the `user_id` decoded
from the JWT, and infers subscription status by watching for `403` responses
from `/rules`. Adding something like:

```python
@router.get('/me')
async def get_me(user: UserModel = Depends(get_current_user)):
    return {
        'username': user.username,
        'profile_pic_url': user.profile_pic_url,
        'next_billing_date': user.subscription.next_billing_date,
    }
```

would let the dashboard show the actual username/avatar and a real "renews on
…" / "trial ends …" date instead of the generic status text.

## 3. Run it locally

Any static server works, e.g.:

```
npx serve .
# or
python3 -m http.server 5500
```

Then open `http://localhost:5500`.

## How auth works here

1. "Connect Instagram" sends the browser to `GET {API_BASE_URL}/user/login`,
   which redirects to Instagram's OAuth screen.
2. Instagram redirects back to `OAUTH_CALLBACK_URL` (must equal the backend's
   `REDIRECT_URI`) with `?code=...`.
3. `login-callback.html` calls `GET /user/instagram_callback?code=...`,
   stores the returned `access_token`/`refresh_token` in `localStorage`, and
   sends the user to `dashboard.html`.
4. Every API call attaches `Authorization: Bearer <access_token>`. On a 401,
   `assets/api.js` silently calls `POST /user/refresh` and retries once
   before giving up and sending the user back to sign in.

## Notes on `/rules` and subscriptions

`GET /rules` (and every other rules endpoint) returns `403` for users without
an active subscription — including a lapsed 7-day trial. The dashboard treats
any `403` from that endpoint as "needs billing" and shows a banner with a
link into `/payments/checkout`, rather than calling a separate status
endpoint (since the API doesn't currently expose one — see above).